+# Agent Note: Web account layer as an out-of-tree plugin over a neutral webserver guard seat
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-14-web-account-layer-plugin.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The Web GUI had no authentication layer at all: the connection plugin's browser-trust fence is explicitly a reachability policy, `dsh web --host 0.0.0.0` is unsupported for that reason, and nothing in the composition could gate `/api` on a user identity. Adding accounts naively would either grow the agent core or scatter auth concepts through the transport packages — and an account feature authored by a fork owner should not masquerade as an in-box `@deepseek-ai` product package.
+
+## Decision
+
+The product change is exactly one platform extension: `dsh-host-webserver` gained a **guard seat** (`registerGuard`), a single-owner admission hook mirroring the existing fallback seat, consulted before every named route, the fallback, and every upgrade. The webserver stays harness-agnostic — the seat carries no auth vocabulary, and with no owner registered, dispatch is unchanged.
+
+The account feature itself is a **third-party plugin maintained outside this repository** — `@night-stars-1/dsh-host-auth` (<https://github.com/Night-stars-1/dsh-auth>), never part of the `@deepseek-ai` release or any shipped bundle. It serves `POST /api/auth/{register,login,logout}` and `GET /api/auth/me` as exact webserver routes (exact beats the connection plugin's `/api` prefix), stores scrypt-hashed users and SHA-256-hashed session tokens in the `web_auth` storage domain, issues the HttpOnly SameSite=Strict `dsh_auth` cookie, serves a self-contained `/login` page, and claims the guard seat to refuse unauthenticated `/api` requests (401) and upgrades and to 302-redirect unauthenticated HTML navigations to `/login`. Loading follows the standard out-of-tree path: `dsh plugin --profile web add <package>` plus an insert row in the profile's `cordis.patch.yml`; the plugin's `@deepseek-ai/*` imports are peers resolved to the host installation's single instances through the profile module fallback.
+
+The pre-session `/api/auth` endpoints carry a plugin-local copy of the connection plugin's browser-trust fence (Host/Origin/Fetch-Metadata), because cookie-based auth must run at the raw HTTP layer where that fence is not applied for exact routes.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Shipping the plugin in-box under `@deepseek-ai` (web-app bundle dependency)** — rejected: the feature is fork-owned, not an upstream product decision; only the neutral seat belongs in the product. The out-of-tree profile mechanism is the supported loading path and keeps the shipped composition untouched.
+- **Typert Remote endpoints or `connection.rpc.handle` channels** — both contracts expose `(endpoint, payload, signal)` only; no request or response headers, so no HttpOnly cookie can be issued or read. Widening those seams with transport headers was rejected as leaking transport into transport-independent contracts.
+- **A browser `ui-auth` slot plugin instead of the served `/login` page** — a `dsh.client` package pulls in the module roster, client bundling, and jsdom coverage machinery for what is one form; the server-rendered page keeps the feature in one folder.
+- **Gating inside the connection plugin** — would put account policy in the transport package and still not cover future non-connection routes; the neutral webserver seat keeps policy in the plugin that owns it.
+- **In-memory session tokens** — persisted (hashed) tokens were chosen so restarts do not sign everyone out; the domain sweep on sign-in bounds the table.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The webserver's public surface grew by one generic seat that any composition (accounts, rate limiting, maintenance mode) can claim; two admission policies cannot compose, which is deliberate. This seat must land upstream before the published `dsh` can host the plugin — the npm `dsh-host-webserver` (`0.0.1-rc.1`) predates it.
+- The account plugin lives outside every repository gate (coverage, invariants, bilingual budgets); its own `package.json` scripts build and test it, with `link:` devDependencies resolving the harness packages from this checkout.
+- Accounts gate access, not data: sessions, workspaces, and settings remain deployment-global. Per-user partitioning, abuse throttling, and account management are recorded in the plugin README.
+- The browser-trust fence now exists in two places (connection, plugin) that must stay behaviorally identical.
